::PC_U04_L07_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U04_L07_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3
Question:
Which identity should be used to rewrite 1-sin^2(x)?
{
~A. tan^2(x)#This identity does not match the expression.
=B. cos^2(x)#Correct. Since sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)=1, 1-sin^2(x)=cos^2(x).
~C. sec^2(x)#This comes from 1+tan^2(x), not the given expression.
~D. sin(x)#This does not preserve the squared relationship.
}
::PC_U04_L07_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U04_L07_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.4
Question:
Which formula gives sin(2x)?
{
~A. sin(x)+cos(x)#Sine does not simplify by adding sine and cosine.
~B. cos^2(x)-sin^2(x)#This is a cosine double-angle form.
~C. sin^2(x)+cos^2(x)#This is the Pythagorean identity, not a double-angle formula.
=D. 2sin(x)cos(x)#Correct. The sine double-angle formula is sin(2x)=2sin(x)cos(x).
}
::PC_U04_L07_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U04_L07_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.5
Question:
Simplify csc(x)sin(x).
{
=A. 1#Correct. Cosecant and sine are reciprocal functions, so their product is 1.
~B. sin^2(x)#This squares sine instead of canceling reciprocal factors.
~C. csc^2(x)#This squares cosecant instead of simplifying.
~D. cos(x)#Cosine is not the product of cosecant and sine.
}
::PC_U04_L07_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U04_L07_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.6
Question:
Solve tan(theta)=1 for 0 degrees <= theta < 180 degrees.
{
~A. 30 degrees#tan(30 degrees) is not 1.
~B. 60 degrees#tan(60 degrees) is not 1.
=C. 45 degrees#Correct. tan(theta)=1 at 45 degrees in this interval.
~D. 135 degrees#tan(135 degrees) is -1, not 1.
}
::PC_U04_L07_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U04_L07_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.3; MLA.PC.TR.4; MLA.PC.TR.5; MLA.PC.TR.6
Question:
Why is identifying the problem type important in an integrated Unit 4 problem?
{
~A. It avoids showing work#Showing work is still required.
=B. It determines which identity, formula, or ratio should be used#Correct. The structure of the problem determines the appropriate method.
~C. It makes all answers positive#Many trigonometric values can be negative depending on quadrant.
~D. It removes context restrictions#Context restrictions must still be checked.
}